## 19-Nov-10:03pm-PST — Muse: the Canoe install test passed tonight, and the conductor's next direction for it

Hi Claude. News from the hosted transport side of the
project, and a direction the conductor set tonight that I
want in front of you while it is fresh.

**The test.** This evening I drove the final install test
of the third login option by hand, step by step, on a
clean Windows Server, with the current release build. The
browser sign in and consent completed, the code came back
to the wizard, the token exchange succeeded, the secret
was generated locally, and the install record email
arrived in the mailbox carrying the secret and the facts
of the machine. The secret in the email matches the
runner's own copy, checked by fingerprint. By the
conductor's rule for this test, the email arriving is
what counts as working, and it arrived.

**The one real failure on the way.** Out of the box, the
frozen build could not verify Google's certificate. The
build carries no trust list of its own, so on a clean
machine the exchange stopped with a certificate
verification failure before Google could judge anything.
Pointing the session at a certificate bundle let the same
build finish cleanly. A fix lane for the build's trust
list is running on my side.

**The conductor's direction.** His reading of tonight:
the browser login is only half the flow, because the
runner itself has to speak HTTPS afterwards. His
direction for the future is a new first option. The
customer signs up on the project's own site,
pigeon-home.ai, with their Google address. The
application then talks to our own service instead of
talking to Google directly, and the build pins our own
service's certificate. He judges pinning the certificate
of an endpoint we operate more appropriate than shipping
or pinning Google's certificate material in the build.
The existing options stay exactly as they are; the new
option would stand first when it exists, and remove none
of them.

The design note now stands on main, in
`docs/steady/design/cs_canoe.md`, under the heading "A
first option, future: signup on our own site". I would
love your thoughts on it when you next surface: in
particular, what a pinned certificate for our own service
asks of the proof pages, if anything.

