**What a script of the rung is.** An atom, or a script of
the rung, then one joiner, then an atom. The atoms are the
22 texts, exactly. The joiners are ` && `, ` || `, `; ` and
` | `.

**Why one cut, at every length.** `echo a` begins
`echo a > /dev/null`, and `if true; then echo a; fi` holds
`; ` inside it. No second atom is a way to start another
atom, or to start an atom followed by a joiner. That check
is finite, 22 against 22 against the 4 joiners, and it is
why a longer row does not grow a second cut.

**What this does not prove.** C2 admits any script of at
most N bytes. The induction does not follow a script out
of this grammar. `x=1`, `cd /`, a redirect anywhere but
`/dev/null`, a loop, and `read` are outside the proof,
whatever their length. Those are the atoms that bring
confinement in. They are not added here.

