## 12-Nov-2:25am-PST — the tag build publishes what the gate judged; the proofs start after the mirrors

3.16.21.7 is the first release promoted whole: the tag build
packaged nothing, read the gate build back (a build.yml run of
main on exactly the tag's commit, every leg green, every artifact
present -- `release_train.py promote-check`), published its
archives, wheel and sdist, and every mirror followed. The tag
build's own work is 2.7 minutes now. The cycles that got there,
the two misses on the way (a REST-placed tag is not in the cut's
checkout; a job's default condition is success() over the whole
upstream chain, which skipped the mirrors beside the skipped legs
on 3.16.21.6) and the floor -- about 8 minutes uncontended -- stand
in docs/steady/reports/ci-failures.md.

Four rules from the same hours, for every seat:

- The proofs start from the tag build's last job, after the
  mirrors, and every proof workflow keeps one concurrency group
  for release tags: the newest tag's run cancels the one before.
  Dispatched at tag time they held the runners the next gate
  needed (the 3.16.21.7 gate build's macOS leg waited eight
  minutes behind three tags' proofs). A tag pushed by hand still
  fires them itself.
- The cut gate and the train read main's push and dispatch runs
  only. A pull_request run on the same sha, or an earlier tag
  build of it, is never the verdict nor the promoted build.
- A push that lands between a release commit and its cut costs
  the tag step (the token tags main's head only; 3.16.21.4 was
  placed by hand for that reason). `release_train.py run` now
  prepares the next version at the head and cuts again on its
  own; the entry for the version that never published stays in
  the changelog with the reason.
- `release_train.py prepare` reads and adds tracked files only;
  an untracked directory in the checkout is neither a change nor
  a commit.

