## 6-Nov-9:34am-PST/PDT — the big-window program: Phase 3 conclusions, and the reconciliation stands complete

This entry posts the Phase 3 conclusions and closes the
big-window conformance program's record. Phase 3 is the
final once-over of everything: the whole suite run fresh
against main, every failure and every skip accounted, and
the remains of the earlier phases stated plainly. The
model mechanics of the program stand paused, so the
once-over was done by hand, from the lane records the
phases left behind; the review work underneath it was
completed by hand, the other way around.

### The suite evidence

The full suite ran against main at b9935cf0: 11,298
tests, 10,820 passed, 55 skipped. Every 1 of the 55
skips was read off the logs and accounted against the
skip registry; no unregistered reason appears anywhere
in the run. The failures and errors were attributed
1 by 1 — matched against the pristine base, proven
environmental, or bisected to the commit that
introduced them.

Exactly 3 genuinely new failures stood in the run, and
a closed bisect traces all 3 to a single commit, the
temp-reaping landing (14b1c416):

1. The Decision-1 startup family table did not carry the
   new work_retention_sec field, so the family no longer
   covered the whole table.
2. The PowerShell script-encoding module staged a job
   and then read the staged script back out of the
   per-job workspace; the reap now removes that
   workspace on every path, so the read found nothing.
3. The Oracle no-widening repair test observed its
   repair through the same workspace, and found nothing
   for the same reason.

All 3, and their sibling casualties in the
campaign-loop, confinement and install-edges tests, were
repaired test-first in 82009eae, and the repair shipped
in release 3.15.17.177: the release's 9 CI guard jobs
are green on every leg (Linux, Windows, macOS), the
release is published, and its stamp verifies. On the
head that shipped, the genuinely new set is closed.

The named new areas stand green in the full run: the
temp-reap tests (21), the hybrid-fuzz families (98), the
core re-pin (30), the skip registry itself, the layout
guard (the release-175 repair), and the keyed-roundtrip
property (the other release-175 repair).

### What remains, stated plainly

The once-over resolves nothing below; each item stands
exactly as it stood, recorded here so the program's
record carries its remains whole:

- 4 held decision items stand for the owner's word: the
  key_policies disposition, the world-readable-secret
  posture call, the POSIX job_user enforce fallback, and
  the posture-only item.
- The unjudged claim sets stand recorded as unjudged:
  lane H 46, lane G 53, lane I 3, lane J 22.
- The live-unproven v3 behaviors stand as recorded:
  resumption over a live mailbox, providers other than
  Gmail, the MIME sender on a live mailbox, a restart
  mid-handshake, and multi-session scale.
- The Independent Submissions Editor publication request
  stands unanswered.
- 2 pre-existing failures, proven identical on the
  pristine base, remain as documented: the agent-stamps
  enforced-window test and the pattern-engine repetition
  test. The TestGrepMaxMatches flake family remains as
  documented too; its counts flap from run to run.

### The releases of the night

Releases 175 and 176 failed their guards and will never
publish. Release 177 is released and verified, and it
carries the whole night's work.

### The program stands complete in the record

With this entry, all 3 phases of the reconciliation
stand complete in the record. Phase 1 covered the
documentation and the codebase; Phase 2 covered the
IETF material, with its remedies standing as the -04
drafts in each draft's source folder and nothing
submitted; Phase 3 is this once-over. The manual review
that follows is the owner's.

## 6-Nov-2:31am-PST/PDT — the big-window program: Phase 1 and Phase 2 conclusions, the 2 improvements passes, and the Phase 3 preparation

This entry closes the record for the first full pass of the
big-window conformance program, posts the improvements pass
that follows each phase, and prepares Phase 3. The program's
setup stands in the 4-Nov-7:42pm entry above the coverage
entry; until now the conclusions and results of the pass
itself stood only in the operator's local lane records and,
for a few items, in the changelog. They stand here now,
whole. Throughout: a mismatch counted only after it was
verified first-hand against the code or the tree, with file
and line evidence; everything else is a hold, a false alarm,
or unjudged, and is recorded as exactly that.

### Phase 1 — conclusions and results (documentation and codebase)

Phase 1 judged the documentation against a stripped core of
the production runner, area by area, in 10 lanes (A to J).
The lane tallies, each recounted from the claim lines
themselves where the model's own summary arithmetic differed:

- Lane A (security posture, present-but-invalid config):
  33 claims, 33 holds, 0 mismatch, 0 unjudged. The lane's
  posture findings carried the substance: 4 verified defect
  groups (the resources and DRI parse errors swallowed in
  the poll loop, PF-1; the job_user enforce pair, PF-2;
  PROTOCOL.md's stale default-off passages, PF-3; a stale
  quoted posture string, PF-4), 2 false alarms, and 1
  housekeeping note verified accurate.
- Lane B (sending and verification): the Argon rerun closed
  at 32 of 32 holds. An earlier flash follow-up resolved 7
  unjudged claims into 1 verified defect (the sending page's
  interpreter wording, since fixed). The verification page
  itself was judged as 1 collapsed claim, not claim by
  claim; that gap is carried into the Phase 3 checklist
  below, not papered over.
- Lane C (overview, networking, extensions): 31 claims,
  23 holds, 1 mismatch, 7 unjudged. The 1 mismatch was a
  verified defect (networking.md calling the Cage opt-in
  after the enforce flip), with 3 further verified posture
  findings beside it.
- Lane D (security review of the guard and wire): sitting 1
  gave 1 confirmed defect (an outside-path existence gate),
  since fixed, and 1 lead that a caller audit later closed;
  sitting 2 gave 2 confirmed findings and 3 false alarms;
  the rerun gave 6 findings — 1 verified defect, 1 confirmed
  low, 1 posture-only item, 2 false alarms, 1 repeat of an
  already-covered item.
- Lane G (the steady shelf, 5 sittings): 360 claims,
  288 holds, 19 mismatch, 53 unjudged. Its verified defects
  include the docker page bundle and the stale-path bundles
  recorded as Q-new-21 to Q-new-23 below.
- Lane H (the infrastructure shelf): sittings 1 and 2 judged
  83 claims, 72 holds, 3 mismatch, 8 unjudged; sittings 3
  to 8 judged 343 claims, 287 holds, 10 mismatch,
  46 unjudged, and all 10 mismatches verified first-hand as
  defects, Q-new-11 to Q-new-20.
- Lane I (root files, CI, extension readmes): 118 claims,
  109 holds, 6 mismatch, 3 unjudged. Its defects include the
  version and SourceForge items of sitting 1, the docker
  entrypoint and pinned-requirement items of sitting 2, and
  the job-watcher severity word of sitting 3, Q-new-24.
- Lane J (the archive shelf): 74 claims, 52 holds,
  0 mismatch, 22 unjudged, the unjudged set being historical
  or frozen record by design.

The fix queue that came out of Phase 1 landed whole:
40 commits, main aa5120ce to 300b6ae7, covering the Q-new-1
to Q-new-27 items in scope, among them the 17 new verified
defects Q-new-11 to Q-new-27 (mostly documentation, 2 in
code). 4 items were held out of the queue on purpose and
stand open as decisions, not as silent drops: Q-new-2 (the
POSIX job_user enforce fallback), Q-new-27 (the sandbox
flag posture item), the Lane D key_policies disposition
(D-2-1), and the world-readable-secret posture call. The
Lane A PF-1 defect (resources and DRI parse errors never
reaching the startup gate) was verified and stands unfixed;
it is named in the Phase 3 checklist below. Phase 1's
conclusion, stated plainly: the documentation and the code
agree in the large — across the lanes, holds outnumber
mismatches by more than 20 to 1 — and where they disagreed,
the disagreement clustered in 3 places: prose written before
the enforce-by-default flip that still describes the old
defaults, stale file paths left behind by the docs
reorganization, and internal process material standing in
reader-facing pages.

### Improvements pass after Phase 1 — proposals, not defects

An improvements pass records suggestions: things that would
make the code or the documentation better but are not
defects, because nothing they describe is wrong today. Each
proposal below is labelled a proposal, carries its reason
and a rough size (S is hours, M is a day or 2, L is the
better part of a week), and stands separate from every
verified defect above and from the fix queue. Adopting any
of them is a fresh decision, not a repair.

- P1-1 (proposal, S). Track the audit line cap in the
  defaulted-settings table. The audit cap's silent default
  is correct and unreachable on the startup path, but it is
  the one default of its family not tracked in
  runner/guard/defaulted.py; tracking it would make the
  defaulted set complete and mechanically auditable.
- P1-2 (proposal, S). Reader-facing housekeeping for 2
  Phase 1 pages: drop the internal milestone labels from
  the SECURITY.md features table, and reword the hunt and
  report references in present-but-invalid-config.md for a
  reader outside the project. Verified accurate as written;
  this is polish, not correction.
- P1-3 (proposal, M). A claim-by-claim packet for the
  verification page, built from the build and CI files the
  page describes. Lane B could only judge that page as 1
  collapsed claim; a proper packet would give it the same
  per-claim treatment the sending page got.
- P1-4 (proposal, S). A mechanical recount tool in the
  audit harness. 2 lanes' model-written summary counts
  disagreed with their own claim lines; a small tool that
  recounts claim lines and checks the summary against them
  removes a whole class of tally error from any future pass.
- P1-5 (proposal, M). A doc-guard test that resolves every
  file path named in a living document. The stale-path
  defect bundles (Q-new-22, Q-new-23 and their cousins) were
  each found by reading; a guard would catch the next
  reorganization's leftovers at commit time.
- P1-6 (proposal, S). A prose guard for pre-flip wording:
  scan living documents for the shapes "off by default",
  "opt-in", and "near zero out of the box" near a security
  feature's name, and flag them for a human look. The flip
  family was the single largest defect cluster in Phase 1;
  the guard keeps it from quietly regrowing.
- P1-7 (proposal, M). Centralize version-string parsing on
  runner/ops/version4.py. 3 separate places were found
  assuming the old 3-component version shape (a release
  script's pattern, an upgrade-proof filter, a workflow
  label); each was corrected where it stood, and 1 shared
  parser would keep the assumption from being made a 4th
  time.
- P1-8 (proposal, M). A CI coverage check for trigger
  paths: a test that maps security-relevant modules to the
  workflow files whose path filters should name them. The
  job-user workflow's filter omits 1 module it exercises;
  the mapping is currently held in people's heads.
- P1-9 (proposal, S). A banner convention for historical
  reports: 1 standard line at the top of a dated report
  saying it is a record of its day and that paths and
  figures in it are as they then stood. Several Phase 1
  sittings spent claims re-deriving that fact page by page.
- P1-10 (proposal, M). Generate the layout page's shelf map
  from the tree instead of maintaining it by hand. The map
  is accurate today only where it was recently corrected;
  a generated section cannot drift.
- P1-11 (proposal, S). Stamp the shipped project snapshot
  with a build stamp of its own instead of a session stamp
  recycled from the working tree, so a reader of the
  snapshot can tell which build made it without asking.
- P1-12 (proposal, S). Put a tracked-file index in every
  audit packet's front matter. In 1 lane the model asked
  for a file at a path that does not exist and corrected
  itself a round later; an index in the packet saves that
  round.

That is 12 proposals for Phase 1: 7 small and 5 medium.
None is a defect; none enters the fix queue by standing
here.

### Phase 2 — conclusions and results (the IETF material)

Phase 2 judged the IETF material under the mirror rule:
the living v1 and v2 drafts as findings only, nothing
edited into them, remedies worded as -04 checklist items;
the v3 draft free to be corrected in its source.

- Lane F, v1: 38 claims, 33 holds, 5 mismatch, 0 unjudged.
  The 5 mismatches were already known from the mirror diff
  and stand in the -04 checklist; the final DTLS packet
  resolved the last 4 unjudged claims as holds and added
  no new item.
- Lane F, v2: 18 claims, 15 holds, 3 mismatch, 0 unjudged,
  on the same basis.
- The -04 checklist stands at 27 items: the 15 known mirror
  differences, the N1 and N2 items, and 10 items the lane
  found beyond the baseline, each verified first-hand. The
  2 code-side fixes the checklist called for have since
  landed; the remaining items are draft words, to be cut
  into -04 from the posted -03 texts.
- Lane E, v3: 49 claims, 49 holds, 0 mismatch, 0 unjudged,
  and idnits at 0 on the stored record. 1 code-side
  observation was recorded beside the verdicts, not as a
  draft mismatch: the email-carriage DTLS server does not
  call the PSK entropy floor. A fresh idnits run has never
  been taken on this machine (the tool is not installed);
  that is carried into the Phase 3 checklist.

Phase 2's conclusion: the v3 draft matches its code on
every claim judged, and the v1 and v2 drafts differ from
their posted texts only in the ways the mirror diff and
this lane together enumerate — 27 -04 items, no more.

### Improvements pass after Phase 2 — proposals, not defects

The same rule as Phase 1: these are suggestions, recorded
separately from the -04 checklist, and nothing here is
edited into a living draft. Sizes as before.

- P2-1 (proposal, L). A worked-examples appendix for the
  v3 draft: a complete DTLS-over-email handshake, with the
  project's own test vectors. The hybrid key agreement's
  RFC publishes no vectors; the project's would be the
  first a reader can check an implementation against.
- P2-2 (proposal, S). A "differences from the previous
  revision" section in each -04 draft, summarizing what
  changed from -03 in the draft's own words, so a reviewer
  of the series does not need the project's checklist to
  follow the revision.
- P2-3 (proposal, M). Shared subject-grammar test vectors:
  1 published set of subjects and verdicts that the v1,
  v2, and v3 implementations all run, since the grammar is
  shared and could drift between them silently.
- P2-4 (proposal, M). A mirror-check script: fetch the
  posted texts, diff them against the living drafts, and
  report, runnable on demand instead of by hand. The
  mirror rule is publication-serious; the check that
  guards it should be 1 command.
- P2-5 (proposal, S). Run idnits in CI over the draft
  sources, so a draft cannot drift out of idnits-clean
  between hand checks.
- P2-6 (proposal, M). A terminology table shared by the
  3 drafts: the door, the shared secret, platform
  disclosure, and the rest, each defined once and used
  identically in all 3, checked in the sentence-review
  manner.
- P2-7 (proposal, S). State the email carriage's entropy
  expectation in the v3 draft's security considerations,
  one way or the other. The code-side observation in
  Lane E is not a mismatch; writing the expectation down
  would make it a checkable claim for the next pass.

That is 7 proposals for Phase 2: 3 small, 3 medium,
1 large. None is a defect, and none belongs to the -04
checklist.

### Phase 3 — preparatory audit (the checklist for the final once-over)

Phase 3 is a final once-over of everything, done by hand.
This is its preparation, not the once-over itself: for each
area, what the first 2 phases already provide as evidence,
and what remains genuinely unchecked, so the once-over
spends itself only where the record is actually thin.

Codebase. Evidence in hand: Lanes A, C, D, G, H, and I
judged the production core area by area; the Phase 1 fix
queue landed in 40 commits with its tests; the do-now work
that followed the ChatGPT and Codex review (duplicate keys
with the nesting pre-scan, the Oracle tool modes with the
environment scrub, the audit hash chain, the posture
assertion switch, the support matrix) landed after and is
not part of Phase 1's tallies. Genuinely unchecked: the
Lane A PF-1 defect, verified and still unfixed; the 4 held
decision items (Q-new-2, Q-new-27, D-2-1, and the
world-readable-secret call), which the once-over must
either settle or carry as named decisions; the 46 unjudged
Lane H claims, 53 unjudged Lane G claims, 3 unjudged Lane I
claims, and 22 unjudged Lane J claims, nearly all resting
on stripped tests, the corpus machinery, or historical
records; and any code written after the fix queue closed,
which no lane has judged. (The send tool's own
duplicate-key check, named here as unchecked when this
entry was first written, was aligned with the shared fix
in the commit directly beneath this entry's.)

Documentation. Evidence in hand: every living document's
title was reviewed and its section headings swept in the
passes that followed Phase 1, with the verified fixes
landed; the Phase 1 conclusions above name the 3 clusters
where doc and code disagreed. Genuinely unchecked: the
verification page claim by claim (proposal P1-3 above);
the historical and archive shelves beyond Lane J's
mechanism spot-checks; and documents created or heavily
edited after the heading sweep, which have had neither a
title nor a heading pass.

IETF drafts. Evidence in hand: Phase 2 whole — v1 at
33 of 38 holds, v2 at 15 of 18, v3 at 49 of 49 with the
-04 checklist at 27 items; both -03 texts verified
byte-for-byte against the IETF archive copies. Genuinely
unchecked: a fresh idnits run on the v3 draft; the -04
drafts themselves, which are not yet cut; and the v3
behaviours no live test has reached — resumption over a
live mailbox, a provider other than Gmail, the MIME sender
on a live mailbox, a restart mid-handshake, and more than
1 session at live scale. The publication request on the
-03 drafts also still stands with the editor, unanswered
in the record.

Tests. Evidence in hand: the fix queue's tests ride with
their fixes; the coverage measure stands at 99.09% of
runner/runner.py (3,938 of 3,974 statements) from the run
recorded above; the doc-guard suites pass apart from 1
known failure that fails identically on a clean tree.
Genuinely unchecked: a full-suite run on the final
once-over tree itself, read whole, with every skip
accounted for — a subset green does not clear this; the
skip registry against whatever that run skips; and the
queued shotgun families 32 to 54, which are background
work and not Phase 3 evidence either way.

Release state. Evidence in hand: releases are cut hourly
when main has changed and verified outlet by outlet, with
3.15.17.173 the latest verified at the writing of this
entry. Genuinely unchecked: the final state of release
3.15.17.172, which was tagged and built clean through its
release step in its hour but was never given a verified
announcement before 173 shipped over it; and the release
that will carry whatever Phase 3 itself changes, which
must be stamp-verified like any other before the program
is called done.

When the once-over has covered the unchecked items above —
or carried each one it leaves as a named, owned decision —
Phase 3 is done, and the program is complete: Phases 1 and
2 judged, both improvements passes posted here, and the
final pass done by hand.

## 5-Nov-12:00am-PST/PDT — runner coverage, measured: 99.09% of runner.py under the unit tests

Coverage of runner/runner.py by the project's unit tests is
measured now, not estimated. The full suite ran under
coverage.py in 8 alphabetical chunks, each chunk with its own
data file, the files combined at the end: 10,156 tests,
31 failures, 9 errors, 55 skipped. The failures sit in the
known families — 16 in the doc-default divergences over grep
max-matches, 11 in the Oracle intake fuzz ring, the rest
singles and pairs, 1 of them a timing assertion at 2.095
seconds against a 2.0 second bound — in a run taken under
coverage tracing with chunks sharing the box. The 55 skips
match the standing baseline, and a skip is not a pass.

For runner/runner.py the combined report counts 3,974
statements: 3,938 covered, 36 missed, 99.09%. The 36 missed
statements are 7 in the file's 3 script-entry dispatches,
which run only when the file is executed rather than
imported, 1 import guard for a sibling module a complete
checkout always has, and 28 failure and guard branches, 3
narrow paths among them that the suite's scenarios do not
reach. No Windows-only branch stands in the missed set: the
Windows pid-liveness block is covered, driven by the tests
with the platform patched. The full report, the per-chunk
verdicts, and the missed-line breakdown are kept with the
run's records, and the measured sentence stands in AGENTS.md
in the core notes, beside the runner's 6,578 lines.

## 4-Nov-8:04pm-PST/PDT — core, the feature: the production runner has a name as a set

The stripped set built for the big-window pass is a named
feature now. Core is the production runner as a set of files,
and runner/ops/core.py carries the canonical list: 130 files,
all under runner/, with a deterministic zip build (entries in
list order, a fixed 1980-01-01 timestamp) and a CLI that
prints the set's file count, line count, zip size and token
counts. The
definition stands in docs/steady/fuzzing.md, where test work
is planned.

The canonical list is the as-assembled set with 1 correction.
The pass set held 133 files; 3 of them —
runner/tools/run_tests_parallel.py, run_tests_shuffled.py
and run_tests_windows_sim.py — exist only to run the unit
tests. They are test harnesses, not runner code, and the
definition of core leaves them out. The recorded measures
keep their names and their sets straight: 619,845 tokens is
the Gemini countTokens measure of the 133-file set as
assembled on 2026-11-04 (zip 701,403 bytes; 54,427 lines,
48,981 of them non-blank — the runner proper,
runner/runner.py, is 6,578 of those lines), and the project
estimator counts
the canonical 130-file list at 566,431 tokens across 53,643
lines (48,293 non-blank). The 2 token counters differ and
neither stands in for the other. The CLI prints the line
count beside the rest, computed live so the figure stays
honest as the set changes, and the tests pin it the same
way. A note in the Secret agents section of AGENTS.md now
tells a visiting system the same fact from its side: the
whole of what runs is about 600,000 tokens, small enough to
read entire and answer whole-code questions from all of it.

runner/tests/test_core.py pins the feature: every listed
file exists, no test file or harness is listed, the list is
sorted and duplicate-free, 2 zip builds are byte-identical,
the zip carries exactly the listed files, the line count is
computed live and pinned against the recorded figures, and
the CLI prints its 5 figure lines in shape. Test core in project talk now
has exactly 1 referent: those 130 files.

## 4-Nov-7:42pm-PST/PDT — the token work, whole: an index, 2 keys, and a conformance program

The reach problem has a working answer now, in 3 parts, and this
entry records all of it in 1 place.

The local index stands first. The tree — 17,716,094 tokens at the
last measure — is cut into 20,176 chunks across 1,439 files and
indexed on this machine: small local embeddings plus ordinary
keyword search, nothing leaving the machine, every query offline.
A 3-chunk answer costs between 362 and 3,086 tokens. The index
finds; it does not hold, and it does not pretend to.

The big window stands second, and it now has 2 keys. The first is
a free Gemini API key, made in Google AI Studio (project
98454394856). Its models list answers, and gemini-3.6-flash
generates on the free tier. The free tier's shape is measured,
not guessed: 20 generate requests per day per project per model,
and the allowance resets at midnight Pacific — a 429 late in the
day named the wait to the minute. gemini-4-argon appears in that
key's models list and answers nothing: its free-tier limit is 0.
A second key, from a billed project (projects/1026122405349, the
Default Gemini Key), went on file on 2026-11-04, and with it
gemini-4-argon generateContent returned a real reply — the model
version in the response reads gemini-4-argon. A key carries its
own project's tier and no other; that is the whole lesson of the
evening's hunt. Both keys live in 0600 files in the operator's
local token-index workspace, outside this repository; no key
value stands in this record or anywhere in the tree.

The program stands third. The documentation and the code are
being checked against each other through the window, claim by
claim. Phase 1 pairs the documentation with a stripped core —
the production runner only, no tests, no superfluous code:
133 files in a 701,403-byte zip, and 619,845 tokens by the
API's own count, which fits the window with room to spare — and
asks, segment by segment, whether the words match the code. The
exchange is conversational: where the window cannot judge a
claim, it names the code or the document it needs, and exactly
that is fetched and sent, round by round, until the claim is
decided or recorded unjudged. Every mismatch claim is verified
first-hand against the code before it counts as a defect. Phase
2 takes the IETF material: the v1 and v2 drafts as findings
only, under the mirror rule, and the v3 draft with verified
defects fixed in its source. Phase 3 is a final once-over of
everything. Between phases, in idle capacity, the same window
reads the code subsystem by subsystem for a security review
under the project's own values.

Results so far, each one checked first-hand. Lane B, the sending
document, round 1: 15 claims — 10 hold, 5 unjudged, every
unjudged 1 waiting on code the packet did not carry (the poll
loop and its 20-second default, the sandbox prefix binding, the
100 MiB download cap, the remote-upgrade kit, the 8-output
ceiling). Lane D, the security review of the guard code, sitting
1: 1 confirmed defect. In runner/guard/extreview.py, the high
finding for an absolute path outside the extension folder is
raised only when that path exists at review time, and the cached
clean verdict is keyed only on the extension folder's own files
— so a file created at the outside path after the review escapes
the cached verdict entirely: a time-of-check/time-of-use shape,
confirmed in the code. 1 further claim from that sitting — a
helper that reads a malformed security shape as off — is true of
the helper and not yet a defect: the startup validation refuses
those shapes before any job runs, and the claim waits on a
caller audit before it counts either way. Lane E, the v3 draft:
the packet counted 64,701 tokens by the API; the window's first
call met the spent free quota, and a first-hand spot check of 15
of the draft's claims found 14 hold and 1 holds-with-note, 0
mismatches. The remaining lanes — the security posture sitting,
the wire-crypto sitting, the v3 pass itself — stopped on the
free tier's daily wall and resume on the billed project's key.

Proud of this one. The tree is 17 million tokens, and the answer
was never a bigger head: it is an index that finds, a window that
reads, and a program that checks the words against the code and
writes down what it finds.

## 4-Nov-4:32pm-PST/PDT — the free big window: a first pass over the documentation

The token work has a second half now, standing next to the local
index. A free Gemini API key — made in Google AI Studio, no card —
is on file in a locked-down store on this machine, and the first
big-window pass over the project's markdown documentation ran
today.

The shape: the documentation reads folder by folder, in sittings
sized to the window's 1,048,576 tokens, each sitting returning a
structured digest — what the part is, the decisions and
invariants it states, anything contradictory or stale it can
substantiate, and answers to the same 5 check questions the local
index was proven against.

Sitting 1 covered the front door: AGENTS.md, PROTOCOL.md,
README.md, ENTERPRISE.md, FAQ.md, INSTALL.md, SECURITY.md, and
docs/FUTURE.md — 59,666 tokens in, measured by the API rather
than estimated, 2,048 tokens of digest out, 65 seconds of
generation. The primary model, gemini-3.6-flash, and the first 2
fallbacks were demand-unavailable; the sitting ran on
gemini-3.5-flash. Sitting 2 — docs/steady/, measured at 382,731
tokens before the call — drew a rate limit on its first
generation attempt, and the pass stopped there under its own
rule: no retries around a 429. The remaining sittings (steady,
infrastructure-documents, ietf, the rest of the tree, archive)
run under the same pattern as the free quota allows. The pass
record — chunk manifests, raw responses, digests — stands at
~/workspace/token-index/gemini-pass/ on this machine.

What the 1 completed sitting is worth, checked against the text
afterwards: the fair-warning question and the author's climbing partner question came
back word-perfect. The version-scheme question came back partial
— the 3rd component (the tree's size in millions of tokens)
right, the rest honestly marked not covered in that sitting. The
DTLS-over-email resumption question and the corpus locking
question were declined as not covered, which was the correct call
for that sitting: their homes are in the folders still queued. On
contradictions, the sitting substantiated none; the 4 staleness
items it flagged — the ENTERPRISE.md placeholder line, the
removed root paths, the removed runner/tests/archive/, and
PROTOCOL.md's open Docker entrypoint item — are each already
marked as such by the documents themselves.

Cost: $0 on the free tier. The same measured usage at paid Flash
rates comes to about 6 cents; a full pass at those rates projects
to about $1.

The conclusion the evidence supports: the free window is real,
it reads whole folders in their own words, and paired with the
local index — index finds, window reads — it answers questions
about this tree in a way neither half answers alone. The pattern
and its limits (the rate limit's pace; the free tier's data-use
term, weighed openly for a public tree; the paid tier as the
honest route for a private one) are written up for visiting
systems in the Secret agents section of AGENTS.md, as a sibling
to the local-index pattern.

## 3-Nov-9:26am-PST/PDT — tasking: what the cores are doing

Snapshot from live process listings on all 3 machines, taken
3-Nov-2026 at about 17:17 UTC. 13 cores in total: 4 on the
Linux VM that hosts this seat, 1 in San Francisco, 8 on the
Windows rig. Every core holds the same standing target: about
90% average CPU, all the time, on real work; an idle core is a
defect. When a release build runs, the lanes ease to about 85%
so the build is never starved. The hard limits outrank the
target everywhere: no disk over 90% used, no paging (available
memory floor 256 MB on the 8 GB box, 128 MB on the small
boxes), no swap.

The rules every tasking runs under: nothing pushes to GitHub
by itself — lanes commit locally and the commits accumulate
for a manual landing; the full flow is in docs/CONTRIBUTING.md
under "Releases: who pushes, and the five-minute window". A
failure found by any tasking is finished only when the failing
case stands as a unit test on main, and a fuzz-found bug gets
a family of tests, not a single regression test. Test output
is read in full, unfiltered, and what skipped is checked every
time. A watchdog checks every lane every 10 minutes and
relaunches whatever died.

Tasking 1, the full test suite in parallel shards, is the
largest. Every runner/tests/test_*.py file is dealt by stride
across 6 shard lanes, each in its own private tree. A shard
runs its files in chunks of 35 modules, re-syncs its tree to
the newest remote main at the top of every pass, and starts
the next pass 5 seconds after the last ends. There is no
finish line: the suite is the regression net, run whole and
repeatedly on every new head so a defect is caught within the
hour. Each lane logs its complete output; a failing chunk's
module names go to the coordinator, which works the failure
in the fix tree — reproduce, test first, fix — and holds the
result as a local commit.

Tasking 2, the rotation lane, cycles the heavyweight batches
the shards do not carry: the security parity and lockdown
batch (the same jobs under no modes, all off, all audit, all
enforce, outcomes compared, plus the startup-refusal tests);
the DTLS batch (the datagram tests, the version 3 carriage
tests, the live-mail fixture replays, the hybrid post-quantum
handshake tests); the transport suites end to end; a corpus
fuzz grow against recent-work targets; and a pyrefly pass.

Tasking 3, the fuzz lanes, runs runner/tools/de_dust_corpus.py
in 12-minute grows, cycling 6 target groups: dtls, dtls_session
and dtls_hostile; keydir, address, wire and crypto; config,
manifest, secret, queries and names; race_board, race_stash
and race_state; mail, board, channels, inbox and local_inbox;
and the version 2 group — v2, v2_mail, board_mail, stage,
collect, stash, local, cloud. The doctrine in the tool: every
input bookended with every ASCII symbol front and back plus
the non-printing edge cases, repetition counts inside the
sweep, and byte-level, protocol and race lanes kept separate,
each with its own corpus. Crashes and survivors bank locally
on the machine that found them and are landed by hand.

Tasking 4 is San Francisco's single core: a suite-shard lane
of its own from /opt/hp-rig/homing-pigeon, working the same
test files in its own rotation — at snapshot time inside the
confinement, coverage and de_dust modules. Its value is a
second, independent machine reaching the same verdicts, so a
failure that only shows on a small, slow, memory-tight box
gets seen at all.

Tasking 5 is the Windows rig's 8 cores: the Windows-side test
and fuzz work that only means anything on Windows — path
handling, PowerShell script handling, the Windows runner
surface — driven by Python workers. The runner itself stands
installed there on the file transport, a consumer of the
tasking's output through the remote-upgrade path, not a
producer of pushes. One honest cost: Windows Defender's
real-time scanner takes a large share of that box while the
work runs, because every file the workers touch is scanned;
the readings below include the scanner.

The coordination tasking uses almost no CPU: the coordinator
that set the lanes up and works the failure queue, the pump
watchdog on its 10-minute cycle keeping the list of local
commits awaiting a manual push, and the release watch, which
does nothing unless the owner edits a document — then it
ships exactly 1 release after 5 quiet minutes.

The readings at snapshot time: the VM at load average 6.35
on 4 cores, 159% of capacity, 6 suite shards plus the DTLS
batch and the fuzz and rotation lanes all on the run at
once, oversubscribed on purpose. San Francisco at about 89%
of its 1 core on the 15-minute average. The Windows rig at
96% of its 8 vCPU, workers and scanner together. The
campaign record lives at ~/workspace/cpu-pump/CAMPAIGN.md on
the VM, with 1 log per lane; a lane is alive when its process
exists and its log is fresh — trust the log.

